  "enhanced_information": "Chemotherapy primarily targets rapidly dividing cells, which explains why certain organs and cell types are more susceptible to its effects. Cardiac myocytes are terminally differentiated and have a very low turnover rate, making them resistant to chemotherapy. In contrast, enterocytes and hematopoietic cells have high turnover rates and are therefore more susceptible to chemotherapeutic agents. Hair follicle cells also divide rapidly and are susceptible to chemotherapy. Liver hepatocytes possess regenerative capacity, but they can still be affected by some chemotherapeutic agents. Thus, the most resistant cell type to chemotherapy among the options provided is cardiac myocytes.",
  "analysis": "Chemotherapeutic agents often target rapidly dividing cells, which is why certain tissues like the gastrointestinal tract (enterocytes) and hair follicles (hair follicle cells) are more susceptible to their effects. In contrast, cardiac myocytes and liver hepatocytes are generally more resistant due to their lower rates of proliferation. Hematopoietic cells, while also affected by chemotherapy, are actively dividing and thus more susceptible. Among the options, cardiac myocytes are the least likely to divide and are therefore the most resistant to chemotherapeutic agents.",
